# DAX Measures Used in the Sales Performance & 2023 Seasonality Report

This file documents the DAX measures and calculated columns used in the final Power BI report for the **Sales Performance & 2023 Seasonality Analysis** project.
The goal of these calculations was to support the main business story:
February 2023 had the highest sales, while April 2023 had the lowest sales. The report investigates whether the difference was caused by customer volume, order volume, average order value, pricing, discounts, or seasonality.

## 1. Sales Measures

### Total Sales USD

```DAX
Total Sales USD =
SUMX(
    'clean_fact_sales',
    'clean_fact_sales'[net_sales_amount] *
    RELATED('clean_dim_currency_exchange'[exchange_rate])
)
```

This measure calculates total net sales converted to USD. It was used as the main revenue metric across the report, including the KPI Overview, Sales Trend Discovery, Peak vs Low Month Investigation, Seasonality Findings, and Forecasting pages.
It allows sales from different currencies to be compared in one consistent reporting currency.

---

### Total Gross Sales USD

```DAX
Total Gross Sales USD =
SUMX(
    'clean_fact_sales',
    'clean_fact_sales'[gross_sales_amount] *
    RELATED('clean_dim_currency_exchange'[exchange_rate])
)
```

This measure calculates gross sales before net price adjustments, converted to USD. It supports discount analysis by giving a base amount to compare against net sales or discount values.
It is useful for understanding the difference between listed sales value and net sales value.

---

### Total Quantity Sold

```DAX
Total Quantity Sold =
SUM('clean_fact_sales'[quantity])
```


This measure calculates the total number of units sold. It was used to understand sales volume and customer/product activity beyond dollar sales.
It helps answer whether sales increased because more units were sold rather than because prices were higher.

---

## 2. Cost and Margin Measures

### Total Cost USD

```DAX
Total Cost USD =
SUMX(
    'clean_fact_sales',
    'clean_fact_sales'[cost_amount] *
    RELATED('clean_dim_currency_exchange'[exchange_rate])
)
```

This measure calculates the total cost of goods sold in USD. It supports profitability analysis and helps compare sales performance against product cost.

---

### Total Margin USD

```DAX
Total Margin USD =
SUMX(
    'clean_fact_sales',
    'clean_fact_sales'[margin_amount] *
    RELATED('clean_dim_currency_exchange'[exchange_rate])
)
```

This measure calculates total profit margin in USD. It was used to understand whether high-sales months were also profitable months.

---

### Margin % USD

```DAX
Margin % USD =
DIVIDE(
    [Total Margin USD],
    [Total Sales USD]
)
```

This measure calculates profitability as a percentage of sales. It was used to compare profitability across time periods without being biased only by sales volume.
For example, a month can have high total sales but a lower margin percentage. This measure helps separate revenue performance from profitability efficiency.

---

### Average Cost per Unit USD

```DAX
Average Cost per Unit USD =
DIVIDE(
    [Total Cost USD],
    [Total Quantity Sold]
)
```

This measure calculates the average cost per unit sold. It was used to investigate whether cost changes could explain changes in sales or profitability.
It helps answer whether higher online sales or product mix changes were connected to lower average product cost.

---

## 3. Order Measures

### Total Orders

```DAX
Total Orders =
DISTINCTCOUNT('clean_fact_sales'[order_key])
```


This measure counts unique orders from the sales fact table. It was used in the Peak vs Low Month Investigation page to compare order volume between February and April.
This was important because the analysis showed that February had stronger order activity than April.

---

### Average Order Value USD

```DAX
Average Order Value USD =
DIVIDE(
    [Total Sales USD],
    [Total Orders]
)
```


This measure calculates the average revenue per order.
It was used to test whether February’s higher sales were caused by customers spending more per order. Since average order value stayed relatively stable, it helped support the conclusion that the sales difference was more likely driven by volume than by larger orders.

---

## 4. Customer Measures

### Total Customers

```DAX
Total Customers =
DISTINCTCOUNT('clean_fact_sales'[customer_key])
```


This measure counts unique customers who made purchases. It was used heavily in the 2023 sales investigation to compare customer activity between February and April.
This helped show that February had stronger customer volume.

---

### Average Sales per Customer

```DAX
Average Sales per Customer =
DIVIDE(
    [Total Sales USD],
    [Total Customers]
)
```


This measure calculates average sales generated per customer.
It was used in the Customer Insights & Targeting page to understand how much customers spent on average and to support customer segment analysis.

---

## 5. Pricing and Discount Measures

### Average Unit Price USD

```DAX
Average Unit Price USD =
DIVIDE(
    SUMX(
        'clean_fact_sales',
        'clean_fact_sales'[quantity] *
        'clean_fact_sales'[unit_price] *
        RELATED('clean_dim_currency_exchange'[exchange_rate])
    ),
    [Total Quantity Sold]
)
```


This measure calculates the average listed unit price in USD.
It was used to investigate whether February’s sales peak was caused by price changes.

---

### Average Net Price USD

```DAX
Average Net Price USD =
DIVIDE(
    SUMX(
        'clean_fact_sales',
        'clean_fact_sales'[quantity] *
        'clean_fact_sales'[net_price] *
        RELATED('clean_dim_currency_exchange'[exchange_rate])
    ),
    [Total Quantity Sold]
)
```


This measure calculates the average actual selling price after pricing adjustments or discounts.
It was used in the Peak vs Low Month Investigation page to test whether February’s sales increase was caused by lower net prices. Since the average net price stayed relatively stable, it helped rule out price drops as the main cause.

---

### Average Discount per Product USD

```DAX
Average Discount per Product USD =
DIVIDE(
    SUMX(
        'clean_fact_sales',
        'clean_fact_sales'[quantity] *
        ('clean_fact_sales'[unit_price] - 'clean_fact_sales'[net_price]) *
        RELATED('clean_dim_currency_exchange'[exchange_rate])
    ),
    [Total Quantity Sold]
)
```


This measure calculates the average discount per unit sold in USD.
It was used to determine whether February’s sales peak was caused by higher discounting. Since average discount did not increase enough to explain the peak, this measure helped support the conclusion that customer and order volume were stronger explanations.

---

### Average Discount %

```DAX
Average Discount % =
DIVIDE(
    [Average Discount per Product USD],
    [Average Unit Price USD]
)
```


This measure calculates the average discount as a percentage of average unit price.
It gives a normalized view of discounting so discounts can be compared even when products have different price levels.

